# Loop behavioral feedback-and-refine cycle — the hands-off loop's
# terminal "done" requires behavioral evidence, and a failed
# behavioral check re-drives bounded verification-gated refinement

The hands-off loop's terminal "done" was, from source
(orchestrator.py:203-244), a SINGLE dispatch + a SINGLE structural-
presence verify (compile.sh + >=1 source file exist) that ran once
with no re-dispatch on failure and no feedback — and the realpb
in-loop check_command was literally "true" (arms.py:200). The loop's
entire definition of "done" was structural presence, architecturally
incapable of moving a behavioral score. This amendment makes the
terminal "done" require a BEHAVIORAL self-check (a self-constructed
functional check derived from the task's plain-language intent, run
against the produced artefact) and, on a failed behavioral check,
RE-DRIVES a BOUNDED number of refine attempts carrying the failure
context back to a sub-agent, under the EXISTING cost/wall ceiling,
VERIFICATION-GATED each iteration (anti-erosion, SlopCodeBench-class
— a binding constraint, not a footnote). On bound exhaustion the
loop emits a definite evidence-backed honest-negative — a first-class
plan-success outcome, reported straight, never retried-to-green, the
bound never weakened.

